## Stage 6 — SOLID Principles in the Tic-Tac-Toe Design

- Every SOLID principle surfaces in a specific, traceable design decision here — not as abstract advice: OCP in practice: the product team wants a "diagonal-only" win rule for a new game variant.
- You write DiagonalOnlyWinStrategy implements WinStrategy .
- Existing Game , Board , and RowColWinStrategy are untouched.
- The open/closed boundary is the WinStrategy interface.

*Applies **design-principles-solid** to the decision above.*

```java
// Game depends on the abstraction — never on a concrete class
class Game {
    private final WinStrategy strategy;

    Game(Board board, List<Player> players, WinStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;   // injected — any WinStrategy works here
    }
}

// The call site decides which concrete strategy is used — Game never knows
Game standard = new Game(board, players, new RowColWinStrategy());
Game variant  = new Game(board, players, new DiagonalOnlyWinStrategy());
```

**Observable outcome:** The open/closed boundary is the WinStrategy interface.

